# Map every author identity used in this repository onto the account that owns
# the remote, so the commits display as that account instead of an unattributed
# grey avatar.
#
# Format:  <proper-name> <proper-email> <commit-name> <commit-email>
#
# The commits below were authored on this machine by local agent identities and
# by an unregistered placeholder address. None of those addresses is registered
# with GitHub.
#
# What this file does, and what it does not:
#
#   DOES    change the author NAME AND EMAIL shown on the commit page, so a
#          reader sees a single consistent author.
#   DOES NOT link the commit to the GitHub account, and DOES NOT make it count
#          toward the contribution graph.
#
# That was measured, not assumed. GitHub decides whether a commit belongs to
# an account by matching the commit's author email against the email addresses
# registered on that account. A .mailmap rewrites the display only. A
# controlled test on a throwaway repository confirmed it: a commit authored
# with an unregistered email added 0 contributions with or without a .mailmap,
# while a commit authored with the registered address added exactly 1.
#
# So these commits still show as unlinked grey avatars on GitHub. Making them
# count requires rewriting history so the author email is the registered one,
# which changes every commit SHA. That was judged not worth it here: this file
# changes no commit content and requires no history rewrite, which is the
# point of it.

